\ Resolve a requested calendar day into its chosen cutlet.
\ The partition tells how many GATE intervals each cutlet occupies.
\ Each gate interval has its own variable day length, so the day
\ inside a cutlet cannot be found by treating spans as equal days.
\ All cutlets have half-open, right-inclusive day boundaries:
\ (previous cutlet gate, current cutlet gate].
\ This routine may recompute gate sauces; do not use its bowls for
\ later seal questions without restoring the shared year sauce.

The requested cutlet number is a number.
The requested day in cutlet is a number.
The cutlet opening day is a calendar day.
The cutlet ordinal number is a number.
The examined cutlet piece is a cutlet piece.

\ Advance by exactly the number of gate spans in a selected cutlet.
\ Keep this loop separate from the loop over cutlets.
To reach the end of one cutlet:
  Put the examined cutlet piece's span length into a spans to cross number.
  Loop.
    If the spans to cross number is 0, break.
    Move to the next positive gate.
    If the gate step budget's exhausted is 1, exit.
    Subtract 1 from the spans to cross number.
  Repeat.

\ The target day must already be inside the chosen year's open/close.
To resolve the cutlet of the target day:
  Put 0 into the requested cutlet number.
  Put 0 into the requested day in cutlet.
  Put the chosen year's opening day into the gate walk's current position.
  Put the chosen year's opening index into the gate walk's current index.
  Put the chosen cutlet pieces' first into the examined cutlet piece.
  Put 1 into the cutlet ordinal number.
  Loop.
    If the examined cutlet piece is nil, break.
    Put the gate walk's current position into the cutlet opening day.
    Reach the end of one cutlet.
    If the target year day is greater than the cutlet opening day,
      examine the closing cutlet boundary.
    If the requested cutlet number is greater than 0, break.
    Put the examined cutlet piece's next into the examined cutlet piece.
    Add 1 to the cutlet ordinal number.
  Repeat.

\ This cutlet is eligible only if target <= its closing gate.
\ The first day after opening gate has day-in-cutlet 1.
To examine the closing cutlet boundary:
  If the target year day is greater than the gate walk's current position, exit.
  Put the cutlet ordinal number into the requested cutlet number.
  Put the target year day into the requested day in cutlet.
  Subtract the cutlet opening day from the requested day in cutlet.
